 "context": [
  {
   "h": "Sacrifice and reading the signs",
   "body": "<p>Romans and Greeks asked the gods' favour before any important step, and marriage was one. Animals were sacrificed, and their entrails, especially the liver, were inspected by a haruspex for signs of the gods' will. A healthy, unblemished liver was a good omen; a deformed one meant the gods were against the plan.</p><p>Dido offers to Ceres, who was linked with laws and with marriage, to Apollo, to Bacchus and above all to Juno, goddess of marriage. She is doing everything correctly, which makes the narrator's comment all the more chilling: no ritual can help a woman whose mind is already lost.</p>"
